# Response headers for the deployed game. Cloudflare Pages reads this file from
# the root of the build output; Vite copies public/ into dist/ verbatim, so
# committing it here is what ships it.
#
# It is applied ONLY by Cloudflare at the edge. Neither `vite dev` nor
# `vite preview` serves these headers on their own, which would leave the policy
# untestable anywhere but production — so vite.config.ts carries a
# `securityHeaders` plugin that parses *this file* and applies it to both local
# servers. The e2e suite therefore runs against the real policy, and the parser
# reading the shipped file rather than a copy of it is the point: a policy
# restated in TypeScript would drift from the one Cloudflare serves.
#
# The PR preview inserts `X-Robots-Tag: noindex` into the `/*` block below at
# deploy time (.github/workflows/pr-preview.yml). A preview is the only place
# the *edge* behaviour of this file can be checked before it reaches
# production — `vite preview` applies the policy, but Cloudflare is the only
# thing that applies these rules the way Cloudflare applies them.
#
# Two things about the format, both learned from a preview deployment rather
# than from reading the docs, and both silent when you get them wrong:
#
#   - Rules with *different* patterns that match the same path are merged, and a
#     header named by both is sent twice. Browsers enforce every CSP they are
#     sent, so two policies intersect: a *looser* rule for a subpath cannot
#     widen the site-wide one, only narrow it further. There is no negation in
#     this format either, so there is no way to carve a path out of `/*`. A page
#     that needs something the policy withholds has to stop needing it — which
#     is why public/next/redirect.js is a file and not an inline <script>.
#   - Two rules with the *same* pattern do not merge: the later one replaces the
#     earlier wholesale. The PR preview therefore edits the `/*` block below
#     rather than adding a second one, which would drop every header here.
#
# This file has no extension, which keeps it out of two things that would
# otherwise object: scripts/check-offline-assets.mjs scans only known text
# extensions, so the `https://` origins below do not trip the "reference nothing
# remote" rule; and the service worker's precache glob does not match it.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://cloudflareinsights.com; worker-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; object-src 'none'
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
